/**
  @page examples-io-mfem MFEM Format I/O
  @brief Building a mesh and saving in MFEM format

  @m_keywords{MFEM, Mesh, GridFunction, Save}

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-io-mfem-intro Introduction

  This example demonstrates how to build a mesh programmatically using the
  mesh builder API and save both the mesh and a grid function in MFEM format.

  @section examples-io-mfem-build Building a Mesh

  The mesh builder provides a step-by-step interface for constructing a mesh
  from scratch:

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh =
    Mesh<Rodin::Context::Local>::Builder()
    .initialize(2)
    .nodes(4)
    .vertex({0, 0})
    .vertex({1, 0})
    .vertex({0, 1})
    .vertex({1, 1})
    .polytope(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {0, 1, 2})
    .polytope(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {1, 3, 2})
    .finalize();
  @endcode

  This creates a 2D mesh with 4 vertices and 2 triangles forming a unit
  square.

  @section examples-io-mfem-gf Creating and Saving a Grid Function

  Once the mesh is created, we can define a finite element space and a grid
  function on it, then save both:

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 fes(mesh);
  GridFunction gf(fes);

  RealFunction c([](const Geometry::Point& p) { return p.x() + p.y(); });
  gf = c;

  mesh.save("output.mesh", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  gf.save("output.gf", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  @endcode

  @section examples-io-mfem-full Full Source Code

  The complete source code is:
  -   @ref IO/MFEM.cpp "MFEM.cpp"

  @section examples-io-mfem-see-also See Also

  - @ref guides-io "I/O in Rodin"
  - @ref guides-io-meshformats "Mesh file formats"

  @example IO/MFEM.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-io-mfem,IO/} @m_footernavigation
  */
